---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import D from "../data/zubrinicroots.json";

const st = (k) => D.fragments.filter((f) => f.state === k);
const anchored = st("anchored"), dated = st("dated"), placed = st("placed"), bare = st("bare");
const dupes = D.dupes || [];
const tier = (t) => dupes.filter((d) => d.tier === t);
const TIERNOTE = {
  "same day": "Identical birth day, month and year. Two people are not born in the same village on the same day with the same given name and the same parents.",
  "same year and village": "Same given name, birth year and village. Strong, but not proof — cousins were named for the same grandfather as a matter of course.",
  "same year only": "Same given name and birth year, no place in either record. Listed for completeness; this is the weakest tier and should not be acted on alone.",
};
---
<Base title="The Žubrinić Fragments"
  eyebrow={`${D.records} records, ${D.real} real people, ${D.fragments.length} loose ends`}
  dek={`This archive has said for months that joining the Žubrinići is the largest piece of work left in it. Counting it properly says something worse and more useful: ${bare.length} of the ${D.fragments.length} loose ends are bare names with neither a date nor a place, and exactly one pair can be joined from what the tree holds.`}>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="fix">
      <h3>Corrected: two numbers this archive published were both wrong</h3>
      <p>The site has been saying <strong>“232 records resolving into 94 separate root
      ancestors”</strong>. Neither figure survives checking.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>232 was too low.</strong> The surname fold split on a
      bracket but not on a slash, so <span class="mono">Žubrinić / Xubrinich</span> and
      <span class="mono">Žubrinić Zubrinic Xubrinich</span> — one surname written several ways in
      one field — never matched the surname list at all. The real figure is
      <strong>{D.records}</strong>. Fixing it brought <strong>214 people</strong> into this archive
      who had been sitting outside it, among them <a href={u("/people/elias-ilija-zubrinic-xubrinich")}>Elias
      Ilija Žubrinić</a> and Casparus Žubrinić — <em>the subjects of two of this archive's own open
      questions</em>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>94 was the right shape for the wrong reason.</strong> The
      true count is <strong>{D.fragments.length}</strong>, and it is high because of something the
      old number never named — see below.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2 id="index">Ninety-two people hang off an alphabet</h2>
    <p class="lede">The tree contains <strong>{D.indexNodes} nodes whose entire given name is a
    single letter</strong> — <span class="mono">M</span>, <span class="mono">J</span>,
    <span class="mono">T</span> — each with a parent called
    <span class="mono">“Žubrinić Zubrinix Xubrinic Zub”</span>, and each holding as
    <em>children</em> the Žubrinići whose names begin with that letter. One of them has
    <strong>26 children</strong>; another has 17.</p>
    <p><strong>That is a filing system, not a family.</strong> Between them these eighteen nodes
    hold <strong>{D.underIndex} people</strong>. Counted as parentage — which is what a naive read
    does — the Žubrinići look joined. They are not: those {D.underIndex} people are attached to the
    letter their name starts with and to nothing else.</p>
    <p class="dek">This archive's own sorting-bucket filter did not catch them, because a bucket is
    normally detected by words like <em>“to be sorted”</em> and these are named
    <span class="mono">A</span> through <span class="mono">W</span>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What is actually left, and what can be done with it</h2>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>How many</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Anchored</strong></td><td class="mono">{anchored.length}</td>
          <td>A year <em>and</em> a place. These are the only ones a register search can start
            from.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dated</td><td class="mono">{dated.length}</td>
          <td>A year and no place. Searchable only if you already know the parish.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Placed</td><td class="mono">{placed.length}</td>
          <td>A place and no year. A century of register to read.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="warn"><strong>Bare</strong></td><td class="mono"><strong>{bare.length}</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Neither.</strong> A name and a surname. Nothing in this archive will ever move
            them, and no amount of work on the tree will either.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>So the honest answer to “can the Žubrinići be joined?” is
    no — not from here.</strong> {bare.length} of {D.fragments.length} have nothing to search on.
    The joining has to come out of the <a href={u("/searched")}>Otočac registers</a>, and this
    archive has read two pages of them.</p>
  </section>

  {D.pairs.length > 0 && (
    <section class="blk">
      <h2>The one pair the tree can offer</h2>
      <table class="reg">
        <tbody>{D.pairs.map((p) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{p.a}</strong> <span class="mono dim">{p.ay}</span></td>
            <td><strong>{p.b}</strong> <span class="mono dim">{p.by}</span></td>
            <td class="dek">{p.why} · {p.village}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Twenty-six years apart at Otočac. It is a lead, not a
      finding, and it is the <em>only</em> one the data supports.</p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>Some of them are not fragments. They are the same person, twice.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Before counting loose ends it is worth asking how many of these records are
    even distinct people. Checking every real Žubrinić against every other on given name, birth
    date and village finds <strong>{dupes.length} groups</strong> holding
    <strong>{D.dupeExtra} surplus records</strong> — people this archive has been counting twice.</p>

    <p>The test is tiered, because a shared name is not a shared person. This archive holds
    <strong>eighteen Ivan Blaževići</strong>, and that is the standing reason it refuses to
    auto-link names anywhere on the site.</p>

    {["same day", "same year and village", "same year only"].map((t) => tier(t).length > 0 && (
      <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
        <h3>{t.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + t.slice(1)} — {tier(t).length} groups</h3>
        <p class="dek">{TIERNOTE[t]}</p>
        <table class="reg">
          <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Where</th><th>Copies</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{tier(t).map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{d.names.join("  ·  ")}</td>
              <td class="mono">{d.bdates.length ? d.bdates.join(" / ") : (d.byear || "—")}</td>
              <td class="dim">{d.village || "—"}</td>
              <td class="mono">{d.n}</td>
            </tr>))}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>))}

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>Two of the five settle it beyond argument</h3>
      <p><strong>Josephus Žubrinić</strong>, born 16 March 1845 at Otočac, appears twice with
      <em>identical parents</em> — Michael Žubrinić and Maria Marković in both. There is nothing
      to distinguish the records at all. <strong>Francisca</strong>, born 6 March 1846, is the
      same story: Thomas and Joanna in one copy, Thomas and Joanna Ratkovich in the other. One
      copy simply knows the mother's surname.</p>
      <p><strong>Alexander</strong>, born 14 March 1839 at Otočac, shows the mechanism plainly.
      The two copies give his mother as <em>Magdalena Oreskovic</em> and <em>Magdalena Mande
      Draženović</em> — and his father as <em>Michael Miho Michaël Žubrinić Xubrinich</em> and
      <em>Michaelis / Michael / Miho / Michaël “Miko” Žubrinić</em>. It is one boy, entered twice,
      under two renderings of the same two parents. Which is the same disease the
      <a href={u("/name")}>spelling</a> pages have been describing all along, arriving from a new
      direction.</p>
      <p><strong>And one of them indicts the index.</strong> <em>Anna Žubrinić</em>, born 5
      September 1809, exists twice: once as the daughter of <em>Gregory Žubrinić</em>, and once
      as the daughter of <span class="mono">A Žubrinić Xubrinich Zubrinic</span> — which is not a
      man. It is the letter <strong>A</strong>, one of the
      eighteen <a href="#index">alphabetical index nodes</a> this page already describes. The
      index is not merely holding people; it is <strong>generating duplicates of them</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Nothing has been merged. This archive
    <a href={u("/method")}>does not edit the tree</a> — it reports what the tree says, and here
    the tree says the same child twice. The count of loose ends on this page is unchanged, because
    only one of these groups touches the {D.fragments.length} fragments; the other sixteen sit
    among the people who already have parents.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The {anchored.length + dated.length + placed.length} that could be searched for</h2>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Where</th><th>Children</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{[...anchored, ...dated, ...placed].map((f) => (
        <tr><td>{f.name}</td><td class="mono">{f.byear || "—"}</td>
          <td class="dim">{f.village || "—"}{f.house ? " " + f.house : ""}</td>
          <td class="mono">{f.children || "—"}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/frontier")}>The Frontier →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/open-questions")}>Question 4c →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/searched")}>What has been searched →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
